{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"unthanking-ermined-springbok","creation_channel":"python","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe plan-review attestation currency check `rebar review-plan <id> --status` is a read-only,\nno-LLM, no-network query that answers \"should I re-gate before I implement?\". It is exported\nfrom the library (`rebar.llm.plan_review_status`) and exposed on the CLI, but there is NO MCP\ntool for it. An MCP-only agent can therefore never ASK whether its attestation is current — it\nonly discovers staleness by attempting a `claim` or a `transition ... closed` and reading the\nresulting error.\n\nFound while sweeping every attestation refusal surface for bug 94a3-1323-3497-4f59, which made\nthe refusal messages name what changed. That sweep helps a CLI agent immediately; an MCP agent\nstill has to provoke a refusal to learn anything, which is the parity gap.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd a read-only MCP tool mirroring `plan_review_status`, alongside the existing read tools in\n`src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py`. It wraps the same `rebar.llm.plan_review_status(ticket_id)` the CLI\ncalls, so there is no second implementation of the check and no risk of the two answers\ndiverging. Give it an `outputSchema` matching the returned\n`{ok, verdict, reason, verified_at_sha, signed_at}` shape, following the pattern the\nneighbouring `verify_signature` tool already uses.\n\nWhy this rather than widening an existing tool: `show_ticket` already carries a derived\n`plan_review_health` payload, but that is relationship/phase health, not the claim-gate\ncurrency verdict — overloading it would conflate two different questions and change an\nexisting tool's contract. A dedicated read-only tool is additive and matches how the CLI\nalready separates the two.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py` — the new read-only tool.\n- `src/rebar/schemas/` — the tool's output schema, if the neighbouring read tools each carry\n  one (follow whatever `verify_signature` does).\n- `docs/mcp-reference.md` — add the tool to the read-tool inventory.\n- `tests/unit/` — a test asserting the tool returns the same dict as\n  `rebar.llm.plan_review_status` for a certified ticket and for an unsigned one.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Happy path: a ticket with a current attestation returns `ok: true`, `verdict: \"certified\"`.\n- Failure path: a ticket with no attestation returns `ok: false`, `verdict: \"unsigned\"`, and\n  the reason naming both remedies.\n- The tool is registered in the MCP read-tool set and is absent from the write set (it must\n  not be gated by readonly mode).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A read-only MCP tool exposes `plan_review_status`; verified by a test asserting the tool\n      is present in the server's read-tool inventory.\n- [ ] For a ticket with a current attestation it returns `ok: true` and `verdict: \"certified\"`;\n      verified by a test.\n- [ ] For a ticket with no attestation it returns `ok: false` and `verdict: \"unsigned\"`;\n      verified by a test.\n- [ ] The tool delegates to `rebar.llm.plan_review_status` rather than reimplementing the\n      check; verified by a test that patches that function and asserts the tool's output\n      changes with it.\n- [ ] `docs/mcp-reference.md` lists the tool; verified by a grep for its name in the file.\n","id":"86c8-4b91-5257-4cca","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"No MCP tool for `review-plan --status`: MCP agents cannot ask if an attestation is current"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785949495508791001,"uuid":"bdb1967e-f32b-4fbb-b3ee-0c6359d4d8de"}